 "research_note": "THE WATER WORKS ENGINE HOUSE, built by T-3689 for the 1871 scene: Andreas's 142 by 60 feet in castellated Gothic, rock-faced ashlar with cut-stone trimmings, a slate roof on massive timbers, the two-storey centre of its long front on Pine Street round the main entrance, two boiler rooms behind with the 130-foot smoke-stack between them (Andreas vol. 2, p. 69). Read off P. B. Greene's stereographs of the works after the fire. Its roof burned at 3:20 a.m. on 9 October and fell on the engines; the walls stood.",
